118 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186256989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259118 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2fdee44b-634e-5d07-a237-7f22717e3786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259310 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259310 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259310 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186257770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259310 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9dcf51f9-209c-5d5a-87f9-c5d00bb116e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259601 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259601 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124166" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259601 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259601 #edb/ref 1071 "The line $y=2x$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x-2},$ for $k\\ne 0,$ only once. What is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259601 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cbecf5f3-3192-5842-9f4d-79b30b51a4fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259601 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=2x$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x-2},$  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x \\\\ y=\\frac{k}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{k}{x-2} \\\\ 2x(x-2) & =k \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x-k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(2)(-k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16+8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =-16 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259753 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259753 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124913" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259753 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259753 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $162+108+72+48+⋯$\n- $1-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{27}{64}+⋯$\n- $-10-9-8.1-7.29-⋯$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259753 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "893c9c3b-88dc-5138-95d4-8126574b5268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259753 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $162+108+72+48+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{108}{162} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $1-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{27}{64}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{4})}{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $-10-9-8.1-7.29-⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{-9}{-10} \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, series I, II, and III converge." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259882 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}5 & \\,\\,\\,-8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 3 & \\,\\,\\,-1 & \\,\\,\\,-8\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259882 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186